{"authored" [{"question" "q-319437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/state.json" "file_sha256" "52865443a90daa78a12f1ed99b43b88f4face2d0677c6cadb7f57a4bd441760a" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-1\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1+2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{k-1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k-1 & =-2 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/state.json" "file_sha256" "52865443a90daa78a12f1ed99b43b88f4face2d0677c6cadb7f57a4bd441760a" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce5bcc711083315f5414ee17801bf085412f4e81564c002496133c9b367a66df" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319437" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/state.json" "file_sha256" "52865443a90daa78a12f1ed99b43b88f4face2d0677c6cadb7f57a4bd441760a" "basis" 848 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979275/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008121/state.json" "file_sha256" "6bdb35a4a91916c18bd3d9d00fa5be36c237dafd79d5fe92bc622d7d61b6eb23" "basis" 1131 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008121/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}4 & k \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(k-2)-3(4-6)+1(4-3k) \\\\  & =2k-4+6+4-3k \\\\  & =6-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-k}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{2}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{6-k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =2(6-k) \\\\ 10 & =12-2k \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008121/state.json" "file_sha256" "6bdb35a4a91916c18bd3d9d00fa5be36c237dafd79d5fe92bc622d7d61b6eb23" "basis" 1131 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008121/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008121/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f90ed32d06641710e2d2ca7b996069a0ece3a9348e3bc6d82247d75ebaea841c" "basis" 1131 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008121/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008121/state.json" "file_sha256" "6bdb35a4a91916c18bd3d9d00fa5be36c237dafd79d5fe92bc622d7d61b6eb23" "basis" 1131 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/1